{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"Rule 6c-11 does **not** require an AP to be a clearing-agency member or to settle through a particular clearing system. It permits an ETF to issue/redeem creation units to an AP that is a **“Authorized Participant” under the rule**: a member or participant of a clearing agency registered with the SEC, with a written agreement with the ETF or its service provider allowing it to place creation/redemption orders. The ETF itself need not be a clearing-agency member.\n\nThat is the rule’s broad eligibility test—not a list of approved banks or agreement templates. A bank’s reviewed U.S. forms may be limited to named counterparties (often the ETF, distributor, or transfer agent) and may impose additional operational, settlement, or documentation conditions. Those are contractual/form limitations, not Rule 6c-11 requirements. Check the applicable ETF documents and the bank’s current forms; don’t infer eligibility from the rule alone."}}
